\documentclass[11pt]{article}
\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}

\title{After Unipolarity: Regional Orders\\and the Division of Labour Among Great Powers}
\author{K.~Abdelal}
\date{}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
The end of the unipolar moment has revived older questions
about how order is produced in a decentralised system.
The classic statement remains \cite{keohane1984}:
regimes created under hegemony can outlive it
because they lower transaction costs and provide information.

\section{Two Logics of Regional Order}
Two-level games explain why regional arrangements
are so hard to sustain domestically \cite{putnam1988}.
Yet the distributional conflicts inside states
are only half of the story;
the control of international agendas shapes
which domestic coalitions can form in the first place
\cite{milner1997}.

\section{Conclusion}
A division of labour among great powers is possible,
but it requires regimes that survive
the power transition that created them.

